Example: facilities for books

Lean properties:speed, robustness, generality, readability, semantic alignment, technical reusability(API quality), legal reusability(license)


Mathlib: Open-source, comprehensive, commuity-built, formal mathematics, Governed by ~30 maintainers and ~50 reviewers, Aims to be a high-quality, high-generality, robust foundation


Mathlib coverage: Mathlib covers almost all the topics in a (French) undergrad degree. It also contains many graduate and some post-graduate topics across mathematics. But it contains $<5 %$ of all "textbook material"

cf. https://leanprover-community.github.io/undergrad.html

Mathlib sustainability: Mathlib started as a volunteer project: contributors, reviewers, maintainers are all volunteers(mostly academic)

As of last year, the community is supported by the Mathlib Initiative, funded by a philanthropic donation by Alex Gerko (CEO of XTX Markets)

The community and the Mathlib Initiative provide reviewer capacity, but this is a scarce and subtractable...

Formal Frontier: Use AI t o autoformalize the "canon" of mathematics. Fill the bookshelves of a formal library, so that prerequisites are available when formalizing state-of-the-art math research

The artifacst are: free, open,  reusable

Formal Frontier is a project of the Mathlib Initiative, funded by an additional donation of XTX Markets.

We are currently building prototypses. Etingof's bok on representation theory, Tao's book on measure theory. Autoformalizing 1 bok costs \$ 10k (depending on the level of maths)

I strongly believe Lean artifacts of the canon of mathematics should be public goods!

We want to inventivize open science, collaboration, and disclosure.

formalization.yaml

A standard for self-reporting: source material, license, AI usage, costs, etc

Hundreds of repositories are already using it.

https://github.com/mathlib-initiative/...

Tau Ceti: A Lean library, written and reviewed by AI. Guided by humans via informal roadmaps. Experimental, started in July. ~100 contributions per day.

#qu()[
  Will this lead to robust and reusable artifacts? Will it scale?
]

Tau Ceti is jointly incubated at the Lean FRO and Mathlib Initiative.

Comparator: Comparator is a trustworthy judge for Lean proofs in an adversarial context.

Describe a claim in Challenge.lean

Provide a proof in Solution.lean

Comparator runs in a sandbox, checks the proof, flags new axioms

https://github.com/leanprover/comparator

Comparator is developed by the Lean FRO

Palomar: Announced 2 days ago! Like the arXiv, but for formal proofs.

Submit a Lean repository, with formalization.yaml and Comparator setup.

Palomar runs Comparator checks, and lightweight review for semantic alignment

Palomar is jointly incubated at the Lean FRO and ICARM

Lean Referee : Referee reads a compiled Lean project and builds a site that answers, for every result in it:what does this claim, what does it rest on, and what would you have to take on faith?

Built by Remy Degenne....

